xpected) | ✅ |\n| 3 | `cp_error_reason`      | `B` (bss)  | 0x008 | `B` (bss)  | 0x008 | ✅ |\n| 4 | `cp_fixed_table`       | `D` (data) | 0x140 | `D` (data) | 0x140 | ✅ |\n| 5 | `cp_permutation_order` | `D` (data) | 0x013 | `D` (data) | 0x013 | ✅ |\n| 6 | `cp_len_extra_bits`    | `D` (data) | 0x01f | `D` (data) | 0x01f | ✅ |\n| 7 | `cp_len_base`          | `D` (data) | 0x07c | `D` (data) | 0x07c | ✅ |\n| 8 | `cp_dist_extra_bits`   | `D` (data) | 0x020 | `D` (data) | 0x020 | ✅ |\n| 9 | `cp_dist_base`         | `D` (data) | 0x080 | `D` (data) | 0x080 | ✅ |\n\n**Symbol diff (C-exported minus Rust-exported): EMPTY.**\nAll 9 dynamic symbols the C `.so` exports are exported by the Rust `.so` with\nthe exact same name, the exact same `nm` class (`T`/`D`/`B`) and — for every\ndata object — the exact same size. Function sizes legitimately differ\n(different code generator); that is not a parity requirement.\n\nData-object *contents* are additionally compared byte-for-byte at run time by\n`tests/data_symbols.rs` (`data_symbols_match`), which loads both `.so`s and\nmemcmp's each array; and `cp_error_reason`'s initial value (NULL) is checked\nthere too.\n\n## Undefined (imported) symbols\n\nC `.so` imports only libc/CRT symbols:\n\n```\n__assert_fail@GLIBC_2.2.5  calloc@GLIBC_2.2.5  free@GLIBC_2.2.5\nmemcmp@GLIBC_2.2.5         memcpy@GLIBC_2.14   memset@GLIBC_2.2.5\nw _ITM_deregisterTMCloneTable  w _ITM_registerTMCloneTable\nw __cxa_finalize@GLIBC_2.2.5   w __gmon_start__\n```\n\nThe Rust `.so` imports only libc/CRT symbols as well (libstd is statically\nlinked). **0 missing / unresolvable non-libc symbols in the Rust `.so`** —\nverified by `ldd -r` reporting no \"undefined symbol\" lines (see\n`check_symbols.sh`).\n\n## `static` (file-local) C functions — `nm` class `t`, NOT exported\n\nThese have internal linkage in the C translation unit, so they are **not** part\nof the ABI surface and cannot be reached across the FFI boundary except\nindirectly through `cp_inflate` / `convert_pix`. They are all present in\n`src/lib.rs` (as private `fn`/`unsafe fn`), so there is **no missing\ntranslation**:\n\n| C `static` symbol | in `src/lib.rs`? | reachable from an exported entry point? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cp_make_pixel_a`   | ✅ `cp_make_pixel_a`   | yes — via `convert_pix` (bpp 2, 4) |\n| `cp_make_pixel`     | ✅ `cp_make_pixel`     | yes — via `convert_pix` (bpp 1, 3) |\n| `cp_would_overflow` | ✅ `cp_would_overflow` | yes — via `cp_inflate` |\n| `cp_ptr`            | ✅ `cp_ptr`            | yes — via `cp_inflate` (stored block) |\n| `cp_peak_bits`      | ✅ `cp_peak_bits`      | yes — via `cp_inflate` |\n| `cp_consume_bits`   | ✅ `cp_consume_bits`   | yes — via `cp_inflate` |\n| `cp_read_bits`      | ✅ `cp_read_bits`      | yes — via `cp_inflate` |\n| `cp_rev16`          | ✅ `cp_rev16`          | yes — via `cp_inflate` |\n| `cp_build`          | ✅ `cp_build`          | yes — via `cp_inflate` (fixed/dynamic) |\n| `cp_stored`         | ✅ `cp_stored`         | yes — via `cp_inflate` (btype 0) |\n| `cp_fixed`          | ✅ `cp_fixed`          | yes — via `cp_inflate` (btype 1) |\n| `cp_decode`         | ✅ `cp_decode`         | yes — via `cp_inflate` (btype 1, 2) |\n| `cp_dynamic`        | ✅ `cp_dynamic`        | yes — via `cp_inflate` (btype 2) |\n| `cp_block`          | ✅ `cp_block`          | yes — via `cp_inflate` (btype 1, 2) |\n| `cp_paeth`          | ✅ `cp_paeth`          | **no** — only called by `cp_unfilter` |\n| `cp_make32`         | ✅ `cp_make32`         | **no** — only called by `cp_chunk`/`cp_find` |\n| `cp_chunk`          | ✅ `cp_chunk`          | **no** — dead code in the C `.so` |\n| `cp_find`           | ✅ `cp_find`           | **no** — dead code in the C `.so` |\n| `cp_unfilter`       | ✅ `cp_unfilter`       | **no** — dead code in the C `.so` |\n\n`cp_image_t` (C `struct`, file-local typedef) is likewise translated\n(`pub struct cp_image_t`). `cp_memcmp` in the Rust source is a private helper\nstanding in for C's libc `memcmp` inside