a_bits`, `cp_len_base`, `cp_dist_extra_bits`, `cp_dist_base` | byte-for-byte comparison of every exported data object between the two `.so`s (and `cp_error_reason == NULL` before any call) | [x] |\n| X2 | `cp_inflate` | after a **successful** call, `cp_error_reason` is unchanged in both (the C never clears it) | [x] |\n| X3 | `cp_inflate` | the whole 128 KiB output arena (not just `out_bytes`) is compared, so any over-write past `out_bytes` must match too | [x] |\n| X4 | `cp_inflate` | the input arena is compared after the call: neither implementation may modify the input | [x] |\n| X5 | `cp_inflate` | R fuzz: 20 000 short pseudo-random byte strings (2..24 bytes) at all 4 alignments, comparing return value, `cp_error_reason` string and the whole output arena; runs in a subprocess pair so `SIGABRT`/`SIGSEGV` outcomes are compared too | [x] |\n| X6 | `cp_inflate` | R fuzz: bit-flipped mutations of *valid* streams (1–3 flipped bits) — same comparison as X5 | [x] |\n\n## Notes on inputs deliberately excluded\n\nTwo input classes make the **C** program execute constructs whose behaviour is\nnot determined by the C abstract machine *and* not determined by the generated\ncode either, so no byte-identical Rust translation is definable:\n\n1. **`cp_dynamic` code-length repeat overrun.** `uint8_t lens[288+32]` is a\n   stack array; a repeat code at `n` close to `nlit+ndst` writes up to\n   `lens[nlit+ndst+136]`, i.e. up to 137 bytes past the array, corrupting the\n   C stack frame (`nlit`, `ndst`, `n`, `s`, the return address …). The Rust\n   uses a padded buffer. Real DEFLATE encoders never emit this.\n2. **`cp_dynamic` code-length symbol 16 at `n == 0`.** C reads `lens[-1]`,\n   which is uninitialised stack memory.\n\nThe fuzz rows X5/X6 keep inputs short (≤ 24 bytes) and mutate only 1–3 bits of\nvalid streams, which cannot reach case 1 (it needs a well-formed 19-symbol\ncode-length tree plus ~300 emitted lengths), and case 2 is filtered by the\nharness (it reports \"skippe