ored of 5 sizes, 4 fixed shapes, `flate2` levels 0/1/2/3/6/9, 2 dynamic), (b) **every** single-bit flip and 6 byte substitutions at every mutable byte (9 168 cases, exhaustive not sampled), (c) 4 306 `in_bytes` lies in both directions and every truncation prefix. Same comparison as X5. | [x] |\")\n\ns = s.replace(\"\"\"The fuzz rows X5/X6 keep inputs short (≤ 24 bytes) and mutate only 1–3 bits of\nvalid streams, which cannot reach case 1 (it needs a well-formed 19-symbol\ncode-length tree plus ~300 emitted lengths), and case 2 is filtered by the\nharness (it reports \"skipped\" rather than a mismatch) — see\n`tests/common/mod.rs::UB_SKIP`.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"Case 1 **was observed**: unrestricted fuzzing of BTYPE-2 headers found seven\ninputs where the last iteration of the code-length loop decodes symbol 18 (a run\nof up to 138 zeros) while `n` is already 267..303, writing as far as `lens[440]`.\nThe C then loops forever; the Rust finishes the loop and trips\n`cp_build`'s `assert(len < 16)`. Those seven inputs are recorded, with the exact\n`HLIT`/`HDIST`/`n` for each, in `tests/fuzz.rs::ub_cp_dynamic_lens_overrun`,\nwhich asserts the only well-defined property: **neither implementation reports\nsuccess**.\n\nThe X5/X6 corpora are therefore shaped so the overrun is impossible *by\nconstruction*, using two rules that are checked in the test source:\n\n1. bits 0..=2 of every fuzzed stream are pinned to BFINAL = 1 plus the intended\n   BTYPE, so exactly one block is ever decoded and no BTYPE can be reached that\n   the case did not choose;\n2. when that BTYPE is 2, the entire `cp_dynamic` header up to and including the\n   last code-length instruction (`Dynamic::header_bits()`) is left intact, so the\n   loop provably emits exactly `nlit + ndst` lengths.\n\nCase 2 is likewise unreachable: the test suite's own code-length RLE encoder\n(`tests/common/deflate.rs::rle_encode`) never emits symbol 16 first, and the\nfuzz corpora never mutate the code-length section.\"\"\")\n\n# Add the extra rows that were actually implemented beyond the original plan\ns = s.replace(\"\"\"| X6 | `cp_inflate` | R fuzz, 25 000 cases:\"\"\",\"\"\"| X6 | `cp_inflate` | R fuzz, 25 000 cases:\"\"\")\ns = s.replace(\"\"\"## Notes on inputs deliberately excluded\"\"\",\"\"\"### Extra rows added while implementing Phase B\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| Y1 | `cp_inflate` | BTYPE 0 followed by *compressed* blocks in one stream. `cp_stored` does NOT advance the bit reader past the stored payload, so those bytes are decoded twice — once memcpy'd to the output and once as the following blocks' bit stream — and the E2 check forces `LEN == in_bytes - 5`. `tests/inflate_dynamic.rs::d12_mixed_block_types` builds exactly that and checks both halves of the output. | [x] |\n| Y2 | `cp_inflate` | every exported table is cross-checked against RFC 1951 independently of the two `.so`s, so a *shared* table mistake cannot hide (`tests/data_symbols.rs::data_symbols_match_rfc1951`). | [x] |\n| Y3 | `cp_inflate` | reference cross-check: for every valid-path row where the result is predictable, the C's decoded bytes are compared against an independent simulator (`tests/common/deflate.rs::simulate`), not just against the Rust. | [x] |\n| Y4 | `convert_pix` | reference cross-check against an independent model of the C loop (`tests/convert_pix.rs::model`). | [x] |\n\n## Notes on inputs deliberately excluded\"\"\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nprint(\"ok\")\nPYEOF\npython3 $TMPDIR/upd_configs.py && grep -c '\\[x\\]' CONFIGS.md && grep -n 'UB_SKIP' CONFIGS.md; echo \"no UB_SKIP left\"","description":"Update CONFIGS.md with real test details"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":986,"cache_read_input_tokens":384407,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":986,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"out