ature power set from `Cargo.toml` and runs everything per configuration |\n\n## Test suite\n\n| file | tests | what it covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `tests/common/mod.rs` | — | loads both `.so`s, page-aligned arenas with identical pseudo-random filler, whole-arena comparison |\n| `tests/common/deflate.rs` | — | a full RFC-1951 **encoder** (bit writer, canonical Huffman, stored/fixed/dynamic block builders, code-length RLE) so each row can construct the exact bit stream it needs |\n| `tests/common/subproc.rs` | — | subprocess/fork driver that compares fatal signals, with a shared watchdog |\n| `tests/data_symbols.rs` | 10 | X1, X2, T1–T8 — exported data objects, incl. patching them at run time |\n| `tests/convert_pix.rs` | 14 | C1–C9, G11–G13 |\n| `tests/inflate_stored.rs` | 9 | S1–S8 + reference cross-check |\n| `tests/inflate_fixed.rs` | 11 | F1–F10 |\n| `tests/inflate_dynamic.rs` | 13 | D1–D13, Y1 |\n| `tests/state_layout.rs` | 4 | `cp_state_t` field-offset probe (see below) |\n| `tests/errors.rs` | 12 | E1–E6, G3–G7, G9 |\n| `tests/aborts.rs` | 13 | A1–A8, G1, G2, G8, G10 |\n| `tests/fuzz.rs` | 8 | X5, X6 (~33 000 randomized cases) + the one excluded UB class |\n| `tests/explore.rs` | 5 (ignored) | diagnostic probes that print the C's actual behaviour |\n\n**90 passing tests per configuration, 4 configurations, 0 failures.**\n\n## What each comparison actually compares\n\nFor `cp_inflate`, per case:\n\n* the `int` return value,\n* the **bytes** of the string `cp_error_reason` points at (the pointers\n  necessarily differ — they are in different `.so`s),\n* the **whole 256 KiB output arena**, not just `out_bytes` — the C writes out of\n  bounds for several inputs it accepts (a stored block whose `LEN` exceeds the\n  remaining input is not rejected), and those writes must match too,\n* the **whole input arena**, to prove neither implementation modifies its input,\n* the fatal signal, when the case kills the process.\n\nBoth implementations always receive an arena at the same alignment **mod 4**\n(the only property of the input pointer `cp_inflate`'s `first_bytes` depends on)\nfilled with the same pseudo-random bytes. That is what makes the C's many\nout-of-bounds *reads* deterministic and therefore comparable at all.\n\nFor `convert_pix`: the whole destination arena and the whole source arena.\n\nWhere the result is predictable, the C's own bytes are additionally checked\nagainst an independent model (`tests/common/deflate.rs::simulate`,\n`tests/convert_pix.rs::model`, `data_symbols_match_rfc1951`) so a *shared*\nmistake cannot hide behind \"C and Rust agree\".\n\n## Divergences found and fixed\n\n| # | where | what the C does | what the Rust did | fix |\n|---|-------|-----------------|--------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `convert_pix`, NULL `dst` with `w,h > 0` | writes through NULL → **SIGSEGV** | Rust's `debug_assertions` null-pointer check panicked → **SIGABRT** | `debug-assertions = false` and `overflow-checks = false` in `[profile.dev]`/`[profile.test]`; every C expression that can overflow rewritten with explicit `wrapping_*` so the profiles agree. Found by `tests/convert_pix.rs::g13_null_pointers_fatal`. |\n| 2 | `cp_find` (lib.c:405) | `png->p += len + 12;` — the `uint32_t` sum is **zero-extended** into the pointer arithmetic | narrowed it to `c_int` first, i.e. **sign-extended**, so a `len` above `INT_MAX - 12` moved the pointer the wrong way by 2 GiB | `len.wrapping_add(12) as isize`. Note `cp_chunk` (lib.c:391) *does* have `int offset = len + 12;`, so the `as c_int` there is correct — the asymmetry is real and is now commented in the source. |\n| 3 | `cp_block`, `cp_chunk`, `cp_find` pointer comparisons | C compares `char *` / `const uint8_t *`, i.e. **unsigned** | compared `as isize`, which differs once an out-of-range length or distance pushes the computed address across the signed boundary | all such comparisons now use `as usize`; `s->out - backwards_distance` uses `(out as usize).wrapping_sub(bd as usize)`, and `c_int as usize` sign-extends, so a negative `backwards_distance` still moves the pointer forward exactly as in C. |\n| 4 | `cp_inflate` allocation failure | `calloc` result is used with **no NULL check** → SIGSEGV | called `handle_alloc_error()` → SIGABRT | the null check was removed so the failure mode matches. |\n| 5 | `cp_inflate` setup arithmetic | `in_bytes * 8`, `in_bytes - first_bytes`, `(in_bytes - first_bytes) / 4`, `in_bytes - last_bytes + i` — plain wrapping `int` | plain Rust `-`/`*`/`/`, which panic under `overflow-checks` | explicit `wrapping_mul` / `wrapping_sub` / `wrapping_div`, so `in_bytes = INT_MIN` behaves identically in every profile. |\n\nTest-harness bugs found along the way (not translation bugs, but they were\nmasking or faking results): `dlopen` is reference-counted so all tests in one\nbinary share a single `cp_error_reason` — the clear→call→read sequence is now\nserialised by `common::lock_globals()`; and the kernel ignores `RLIMIT_CORE`\nwhen `core_pattern` is a pipe, so the forked grandchild now traps the fatal\nsignals itself instead of handing 3 000 cores to `systemd-coredump` (157 s → 6.6 s).\n\n## Structural equivalence of the private `cp_state_t`\n\n`cp_decode` reads `tree[lo - 1]`, and `lo` is `0` when the tree is empty, so with\n`tree == s->len` it reads **`s->dst[31]`** — the C's behaviour depends on the\nexact field offsets of a struct that is never exposed.\n\n`tests/state_layout.rs` pins this down *discriminatingly*: it builds a two-block\nstream whose first block gives distance symbol 15 the only length-6 code (so\n`cp_build` writes `s->dst[31] = 0xF800_00F6`) and whose second block has an\nall-zero code-length alphabet, so all 258 code lengths are decoded from\n`s->len[-1]`. That value decodes to code-length symbol **15**, an ordinary\nliteral length, so the call *succeeds* and returns real bytes — any other value\nat `s->len[-1]` would give a different symbol and a different outcome. A second\ntest shows `s->dst` is not reset between blocks, so `s->dst[31]` survives an\nintervening `HDIST = 1` block.\n\n## The exported tables are runtime inputs\n\n`cp_fixed_table`, `cp_permutation_order`, `cp_len_extra_bits`, `cp_len_base`,\n`cp_dist_extra_bits` and `cp_dist_base` are writable `D`-class objects that\n`cp_fixed` / `cp_dynamic` / `cp_block` read at run time. `CONFIGS.md` rows\nT1–T8 patch them identically in both `.so`s and check `cp_inflate` reacts\nidentically. These are the only rows that can distinguish \"the Rust\ndereferences the exported global\" from \"the Rust constant-folded a private\ncopy\" — with the tables at their default values the two are indistinguishable.\n\n## Source-level audit of the code no test can reach\n\n`cp_paeth`, `cp_make32`, `cp_chunk`, `cp_find` and `cp_unfilter` are `static`\n**and have no caller** in `c_src/src/lib.c`, so they are dead code in the C\n`.so` and unreachable across the FFI boundary. They were audited line by line\ninstead; divergences 2 and 3 above came out of that audit. The remaining points\nchecked and found equivalent:\n\n* `cp_paeth` — `int p = a + b - c` with `a,b,c` promoted from `uint8_t`, three\n  `abs()` calls, and the nested conditional's exact tie-breaking (`pa <= pb &&\n  pa <= pc`, then `pb <= pc`).\n* `cp_make32` — `s[0] << 24` overflows `int` in C; the Rust computes the same\n  bit pattern in `u32`.\n* `cp_unfilter` — all five filter cases for row 0 **and** for rows `y > 0`,\n  including the C's quirks: row 0's cases 1/3/4 start at `x = bpp` and use `0`\n  where later rows use `prev[x]`; case 2 for row 0 is a no-op; case 2 for\n  `y > 0` has two loops with identical bodies; case 1 for `y > 0` has a\n  `raw[x] += 0` loop; the second loop of each case *continues* from the `x` the\n  first loop left; and the `for` increment `y++, prev = raw, raw += len` runs\n  after `*raw++` has already advanced `raw` by one.\n* `cp_unfilter`'s `default: return 0` returns before the increment clause.\n* `uint8_t += ` wraps mod 256 (`wrapping_add`), and `(raw[x-bpp] + prev[x]) / 2`\n  is computed in `int` before being truncated.\n\n## Excluded: one input class with no definable translation\n\n`cp_dynamic` decodes `nlit + ndst` code lengths into `uint8_t lens[288 + 32]`, a\n**C stack array**. A repeat code (16/17/18) starting near the end of that range\nwrites up to 137 bytes past it, over `nlit`, `ndst`, `n`, `s`, the saved\nregisters and the return address. The result is a property of gcc's `-O0` frame\nlayout, not of the C source, so there is no byte-identical translation to write\n(the Rust keeps the writes inside a padded buffer and lets the loop finish).\n\nUnrestricted fuzzing *did* find this: seven concrete inputs are recorded in\n`tests/fuzz.rs::ub_cp_dynamic_lens_overrun` together with the exact\n`HLIT`/`HDIST`/`n` for each (diagnosed against an instrumented copy of `lib.c`\nbuilt outside `c_src/`). That test asserts the only well-defined property:\n**neither implementation ever reports success**. The X5/X6 corpora are shaped so\nthe overrun is impossible by construction — see the two rules in the\n`tests/fuzz.rs` module documentation.\n\nThe related case, code-length symbol 16 at `n == 0` reading `lens[-1]`\n(uninitialised C stack), is likewise never generated.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- [x] `SYMBOLS.md`: all 9 dynamic symbols the C `.so` exports are exported by\n      the Rust `.so` with identical names, `nm` classes and data-object sizes;\n      symbol diff is EMPTY; `ldd -r` reports no undefined symbols.\n- [x] Phase B: every row in `CONFIGS.md` (C1–C9, S1–S8, F1–F10, D1–D13, T1–T8,\n      X1–X6, Y1–Y4) passes across randomized inputs.\n- [x] Phase C: every row in `ERRORS.md` (E1–E6, A1–A8, G1–G13) has a passing\n      differential test asserting the *same* error code / sentinel / signal, or\n      a proof of unreachability (A9, A10, A11, E12–E14).\n- [x] Phase D: all of the above hold under every configuration —\n      default, `--no-default-features`, `--all-features` and `--release`\n      (`./run_all_configs.sh` → `ALL CONFIGURATIONS PASSED`).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
